1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,950
Lesson 15: the Optimizer

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:15,978
YA-NEC course, lesson 15: the Optimizer. First the DK7ZB 6-element Yagi of lesson 14, from 28 to 50 ohm; then a 3-element HF Yagi.

3
00:00:16,028 --> 00:01:55,936
New model, 144.300 MHz. This time the reflector, driven element and first director use variables: LR, LDE, LD1 and their positions XDE, XD1.

4
00:01:55,986 --> 00:02:07,873
Starting point, the designer's dimensions: 30.2 - j3.5 ohm, SWR 1.67 on 50 ohm, 12.06 dBi, F/B 30 dB.

5
00:02:07,923 --> 00:02:16,003
Variables and optimization tab, Optimizer.

6
00:02:16,053 --> 00:02:28,741
On the left the goals and their weights: SWR at most 1.3 on Z0 = 50 ohm, gain as high as possible, F/B at least 22 dB, wanted impedance Z0 + j0.

7
00:02:28,791 --> 00:02:43,383
In the middle the variables it may change, each with minimum, maximum and step; on the left, below the goals, the three frequencies to respect: 143.55, 144.3 and 145.05 MHz.

8
00:02:43,433 --> 00:02:53,383
Below the variables, Monte Carlo: 8 random starts, then the 3 best candidates checked with 50 copies that simulate building tolerances.

9
00:02:53,433 --> 00:02:57,459
'Start'.

10
00:02:57,509 --> 00:03:14,095
1204 steps in 35 seconds, tolerances included: 40.4 + j0.6 ohm, SWR 1.24 at band centre and 1.30 at the worst edge, 11.99 dBi, F/B 33.6 dB.

11
00:03:14,145 --> 00:03:28,956
The price of direct 50 ohm feed: 0.07 dB of gain. The driven element grew 1 cm, the first director and the reflector got shorter. With the tolerances 58 % of the copies stay within the thresholds.

12
00:03:29,006 --> 00:03:35,570
'Apply to model': the new values go into Variables.

13
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:44,571
The model now matches 50 ohm without the 28 ohm transformer. The step log lets you go back to any intermediate solution.

14
00:03:44,621 --> 00:05:07,581
Now HF: New, a 3-element Yagi for 14.150 MHz from the rule-of-thumb dimensions.

15
00:05:07,631 --> 00:05:26,354
Reflector 0.51, driven element 0.47, director 0.45 wavelengths, spacings 0.15 and 0.3 wavelengths, 30 mm tubes: 26.4 - j11 ohm, 7.85 dBi, F/B 21.6 dB.

16
00:05:26,404 --> 00:05:38,654
Optimizer, same goals, start.

17
00:05:38,704 --> 00:05:46,155
The vote climbs fast and then stabilizes: the optimizer tries one variable at a time, starting again from eight random points.

18
00:05:46,205 --> 00:05:58,747
Result: F/B from 21.6 to 32.8 dB, gain 7.75 dBi, impedance about 33 ohm, SWR 1.53 on 50 ohm.

19
00:05:58,797 --> 00:06:12,947
A 3-element Yagi with a deep rear null naturally sits near 25-35 ohm: that is why no Monte Carlo copy stays within SWR 1.3. It needs a hairpin or a gamma match - lesson 12 shows how to compute them.

20
00:06:12,997 --> 00:06:24,543
Goals, weights, variables, frequencies: the Optimizer does in seconds what took evenings of trimming. Next lesson: advanced VHF/UHF. 73 de IC8TEM
